1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
You can't see water vapour leaving a leaf.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:05,120
But you can watch a tiny air bubble

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,600
creep along a glass tube as a leafy

4
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,120
shoot draws water in.

5
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,160
In still air, it moves about four millimetres

6
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:12,960
a minute.

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:14,960
With a fan on, it moves more than

8
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,079
twice as fast.

9
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,640
So how do you test this fairly, and

10
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,640
what does the bubble really tell you?

11
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:24,106
Recall how plants lose water.

12
00:00:24,427 --> 00:00:27,546
Water evaporates inside the leaves and diffuses out

13
00:00:27,627 --> 00:00:30,266
through the stomata, and more water is pulled

14
00:00:30,347 --> 00:00:32,506
up the xylem to replace it.

15
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:35,386
So the faster a leafy shoot loses water,

16
00:00:35,467 --> 00:00:38,266
the faster its stem draws water in.

17
00:00:38,307 --> 00:00:40,826
Measuring what goes in lets you estimate what

18
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:41,626
comes out.

19
00:00:42,834 --> 00:00:45,393
That's the job of a potometer, a device

20
00:00:45,474 --> 00:00:47,553
that measures how fast a shoot takes up

21
00:00:47,634 --> 00:00:48,033
water.

22
00:00:48,674 --> 00:00:50,593
The cut shoot is sealed into a tube

23
00:00:50,674 --> 00:00:53,233
of water, joined to a narrow glass tube

24
00:00:53,314 --> 00:00:54,033
with a scale.

25
00:00:54,674 --> 00:00:57,073
As the shoot draws water in, the water

26
00:00:57,154 --> 00:00:59,793
column moves towards it, carrying a small air

27
00:00:59,874 --> 00:01:00,593
bubble along.

28
00:01:01,154 --> 00:01:02,993
The distance the bubble moves in a set

29
00:01:03,074 --> 00:01:04,993
time gives the rate of uptake.

30
00:01:05,554 --> 00:01:07,633
A reservoir lets you push the bubble back

31
00:01:07,714 --> 00:01:08,513
to the start.

32
00:01:09,734 --> 00:01:12,213
Any leak means the bubble no longer tracks

33
00:01:12,294 --> 00:01:14,773
what the shoot takes in, so the set-up

34
00:01:14,934 --> 00:01:16,333
must be airtight.

35
00:01:16,614 --> 00:01:19,093
Cut the shoot under water, so air can't

36
00:01:19,214 --> 00:01:21,253
enter the xylem and block it, and fit

37
00:01:21,334 --> 00:01:23,093
it in under water too.

38
00:01:23,134 --> 00:01:25,573
Seal the joints with petroleum jelly.

39
00:01:25,774 --> 00:01:27,893
Then lift the narrow tube's end out of

40
00:01:28,014 --> 00:01:30,453
its water for a moment, so one small

41
00:01:30,534 --> 00:01:32,933
bubble enters, far from the shoot.

42
00:01:33,014 --> 00:01:35,173
Let the shoot settle for about five minutes

43
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:36,133
before timing.

44
00:01:37,334 --> 00:01:37,973
The aim:

45
00:01:38,534 --> 00:01:40,853
find out how air movement affects the rate

46
00:01:40,934 --> 00:01:43,333
of water uptake by a leafy shoot.

47
00:01:43,654 --> 00:01:47,013
The independent variable is air movement, fan off

48
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:48,133
or fan on.

49
00:01:48,614 --> 00:01:51,413
The dependent variable is the rate, how far

50
00:01:51,494 --> 00:01:53,093
the bubble moves per minute.

51
00:01:53,334 --> 00:01:56,213
Keep everything else the same: the same shoot,

52
00:01:56,334 --> 00:01:58,693
so the leaf area can't change, the same

53
00:01:58,774 --> 00:02:01,813
room, temperature and light, and the same fan

54
00:02:01,894 --> 00:02:02,693
distance.

55
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:07,500
Still air is the control, the comparison without

56
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:08,380
the fan.

57
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:11,020
Time the bubble over five minutes, push it

58
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:13,980
back, and repeat three times for each condition.

59
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:17,260
Alternate still and fan trials, and let the

60
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:20,220
shoot settle after each switch before timing.

61
00:02:21,434 --> 00:02:22,473
Here's one reading.

62
00:02:22,954 --> 00:02:25,593
In still air, the bubble moved twenty one

63
00:02:25,674 --> 00:02:27,513
millimetres in five minutes.

64
00:02:28,154 --> 00:02:31,273
Rate is distance divided by time, so that's

65
00:02:31,434 --> 00:02:33,953
four point two millimetres per minute.

66
00:02:34,394 --> 00:02:36,233
Read the same edge of the bubble each

67
00:02:36,314 --> 00:02:38,793
time, with your eye level with the tube.

68
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,360
Here are all six.

69
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:44,480
In still air, four point two, three point

70
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:47,040
eight and four point oh, a mean of

71
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:47,720
four.

72
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,880
With the fan, nine point four, nine point

73
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:53,440
two and nine point six, a mean of

74
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:54,880
nine point four.

75
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:57,360
The repeats agree within about half a millimetre

76
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,360
per minute, and the gap is more than

77
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:00,240
five.

78
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:02,480
So with the fan, this shoot took up

79
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,200
water a little more than twice as fast.

80
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:07,520
Moving air sweeps the moist air from the

81
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,800
leaf surface, so vapour diffuses out faster.

82
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,680
Be careful what you claim.

83
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,960
A potometer measures water uptake, not water loss.

84
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:20,240
Most of the water taken in is lost

85
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:22,960
by transpiration, but a little is used in

86
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:25,760
photosynthesis or stays in the cells.

87
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:27,680
And this is one cut shoot with no

88
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:30,000
roots, not a whole plant.

89
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:33,480
So keep the conclusion narrow: with moving air,

90
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,680
this shoot took up water faster than in

91
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:36,480
still air.

92
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:40,900
A second method asks a different question: where

93
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:42,500
can water leave?

94
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:45,220
Paint clear nail varnish on a leaf, let

95
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,300
it dry, and lift it off with clear

96
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:48,260
tape.

97
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:51,380
This peel keeps an imprint of the surface.

98
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:55,220
Under a microscope, at one magnification, count stomata

99
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:57,940
in several random fields on the lower surface,

100
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:00,500
then the upper, across a few leaves.

101
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:02,820
A count alone can't tell how much water

102
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:03,780
is being lost.

103
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,440
Now judge it with three questions.

104
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:10,200
Validity: does it measure what it was meant

105
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:10,800
to?

106
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:15,560
Controls support it: one settled shoot, alternating trials,

107
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,400
steady temperature and light.

108
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:20,040
It tests uptake, which is close to water

109
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:21,680
loss but not the same.

110
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:24,600
Reliability: do repeats agree?

111
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:27,520
Yes, within half a millimetre per minute.

112
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,080
Accuracy: how close to the true value?

113
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:33,800
A slow leak shifts every reading, and more

114
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:35,560
repeats can't fix a leak.

115
00:04:36,767 --> 00:04:38,206
The risk assessment.

116
00:04:38,527 --> 00:04:40,766
Cut stems on a tile, away from your

117
00:04:40,847 --> 00:04:43,646
fingers, because a sharp blade can slip.

118
00:04:43,967 --> 00:04:46,046
Keep the fan and its cord away from

119
00:04:46,127 --> 00:04:48,606
the water, and mop up spills, to avoid

120
00:04:48,647 --> 00:04:49,646
an electric shock.

121
00:04:50,207 --> 00:04:53,566
Nail varnish is flammable and its fumes irritate,

122
00:04:53,647 --> 00:04:56,046
so use a little, in a ventilated room,

123
00:04:56,207 --> 00:04:57,486
away from flames.

124
00:04:58,700 --> 00:04:59,340
Your turn.

125
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:02,380
A student used a potometer on one leafy

126
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:05,660
shoot, with and without a clear plastic bag

127
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:06,940
over its leaves.

128
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:10,300
Without the bag, the bubble moved thirty millimetres

129
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:11,420
in six minutes.

130
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:14,700
With the bag, it moved twelve millimetres in

131
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:15,500
six minutes.

132
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:19,540
Calculate both rates, then explain the difference.

133
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:21,180
Pause here to answer.

134
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:34,560
Thirty divided by six is five millimetres per

135
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:37,440
minute without the bag, and twelve divided by

136
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:39,360
six is two with it.

137
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:42,640
Inside the bag, water vapour builds up, so

138
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:45,520
the air around the leaves gets very humid.

139
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:47,920
The vapour difference between leaf and air is

140
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:51,280
smaller, so vapour diffuses out more slowly, and

141
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:53,360
the shoot takes up less.

142
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:56,080
The classic mistake is saying the bag blocks

143
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:57,120
the water.

144
00:05:57,240 --> 00:05:58,880
It traps humid air.

145
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:00,940
One more.

146
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,060
A student cut a shoot in the air

147
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:05,300
and pushed it into a potometer.

148
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:06,900
She measured the rate with a fan on

149
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:09,700
a warm Monday afternoon, and in still air

150
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:12,820
on a cool Tuesday morning, then concluded that

151
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:15,620
wind increases transpiration.

152
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:18,740
Identify two problems, and propose a change that

153
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:20,420
fixes each one.

154
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:21,780
Pause here to answer.

155
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,880
One: cutting in air lets air into the

156
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:37,880
xylem, which can block uptake, so cut and

157
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:39,720
fit the shoot under water.

158
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:42,440
Two: the temperature changed as well as the

159
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:44,600
fan, so the difference could come from the

160
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:45,640
temperature.

161
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:47,800
Test both with the same shoot, in the

162
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:50,120
same room, on the same day.

163
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:52,280
The classic mistake is to repeat it more

164
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:53,240
times.

165
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:56,040
Repeats help reliability, but they can't fix an

166
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:57,560
uncontrolled variable.

167
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:02,846
So for any transpiration practical: change one factor,

168
00:07:02,967 --> 00:07:06,046
keep everything else the same, seal it airtight,

169
00:07:06,247 --> 00:07:08,686
repeat each reading, and claim only what you

170
00:07:08,767 --> 00:07:11,166
measured, the uptake by this shoot.

171
